.TH MINSEC-ID 1 "2026-10-26" "minsec-id" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
minsec-id \- minimalist host intrusion detection for modern Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B minsec-id
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.RI [ command-options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B minsec-id
checks a host for signs of intrusion using what the system already records,
instead of keeping a database of its own.
File contents are compared with the digests the package manager recorded
(rpm or dpkg); kernel state comes from
.IR /proc ,
.IR /sys ,
and the
.BR bpf (2)
and
.BR sock_diag (7)
interfaces.
.PP
Scans are run by systemd timers: a quick scan every 30 minutes and a full scan
daily.
Nothing stays resident between scans, unless the optional eBPF monitor
.RB ( "minsec-id daemon" ,
.BR minsec-id.service )
is enabled; see
.BR DAEMON .
A file is hashed only when there is evidence it changed (its ctime is newer
than its package's install time, or its size or mtime differ), or when
it falls in the day's slice of a rolling sample that covers every packaged
file once per
.B scan.sample_days
days.
.PP
Each check has a stable id.
A finding is reported once, when first seen or when it changes, and stays open
until a later run of its check no longer sees it; it is then logged as
resolved.
Checks that compare against a baseline record it on their first run; later
runs report changes until they are reverted or the baseline is reset with
.BR "minsec-id baseline \-\-reset" .
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c " " \fIdirectory\fR ", " \-\-config-dir " " \fIdirectory\fR
Use
.I directory
as the configuration directory instead of
.IR /etc/minsec-id .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write one machine-readable JSON document to standard output.
Documents describing a scan or inspection carry a
.B schema_version
field.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the program version and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "scan [--quick | --full] [--check id]... [-q]"
Run the enabled checks.
.B \-\-quick
(the default) runs checks that read only
.IR /proc ,
.IR /sys ,
metadata, and small files;
.B \-\-full
adds checks that read file contents at scale.
.B \-\-check
runs only the named checks, enabled or not.
New and resolved findings go to the findings log, to journald (unless
disabled), and to the notify hook.
.B \-q
prints nothing.
.SS status
The last scan, the open findings by severity, the daemon's recent findings,
and, when it is running, its uptime, attached probes, the kernel records it
examined (execs and writes, not findings), records lost, and memory.
Show the last scan and counts of open findings by severity.
.SS "findings [--all] [--severity level]"
List open findings, most severe first.
.B \-\-all
includes accepted findings.
.SS "show finding"
Show one finding, by fingerprint or
.IR check : subject .
.SS "accept finding [--reason text]"
Write an accept rule for a finding to
.IR conf.d/accept.toml .
The rule is keyed
.IR check : subject ,
so it covers future changes to the same subject; a subject ending in
.B *
matches by prefix.
Accepted findings are recorded but not reported.
.SS "unaccept key"
Remove an accept rule from
.IR conf.d/accept.toml .
.SS "verify path | package"
Hash one packaged file, or every file of one package, and compare it with
the package database, configuration files included.
.SS "baseline [--reset] [--check id]..."
List the baselines recorded by checks, or, with
.BR \-\-reset ,
forget them so the next scan records the current state.
Reset after reviewing a reported change you want to keep.
.SS checks
List checks and daemon rules: an asterisk marks enabled ones; each shows its
cost
.RB ( quick ,
.BR full ,
or
.B daemon
for a rule) and whether it can run on this host.
.SS "enable id" ", " "disable id"
Enable or disable a check or daemon rule: writes
.IR conf.d/ id .toml .
A rule enabled while the daemon runs takes effect when it restarts.
.SS daemon
Run the eBPF monitor in the foreground until SIGTERM or SIGINT.
SIGHUP (or
.BR "systemctl reload minsec-id" )
re-reads the configuration.
.SS inspect
Report kernel features (BTF, BPF LSM, IMA, lockdown, Secure Boot), the active
LSMs, the package managers present, whether the host boots from ostree, the
configuration sources, and the checks.
.SS check
Validate the configuration.
.SS "events [-n count]"
Print the last records of the findings log (JSON Lines).
.SH CHECKS
.TP
.B kernel-tainted
Kernel taint flags, with the modules responsible.
.TP
.BR kmod-unowned ", " kmod-hidden
Loaded modules no package installed; modules missing from one of
.IR /proc/modules ,
.IR /sys/module ,
and
.IR /proc/kallsyms .
.TP
.B bpf-prog-unknown
Loaded eBPF programs of tracing, kprobe, and LSM types.
.TP
.B ftrace-hooks
Kernel functions hooked through ftrace, grouped by owner.
.TP
.BR boot-posture " (emits " boot-cmdline )
Lockdown, Secure Boot, module signature enforcement, SELinux and AppArmor
modes, and hardening sysctls getting weaker than the baseline; command-line
parameters that disable protections.
.TP
.B boot-kernel
Kernel images in
.I /boot
compared with their packaged copies (full scans).
.TP
.BR pkg-signatures " (emits " pkg-db-tampered ", " pkg-unsigned )
rpm header signature failures, which mean the rpm database was edited, and
unsigned packages.
.TP
.BR pkg-verify " (emits " pkg-modified ", " pkg-missing ", " pkg-mode-changed ", " pkg-config-modified )
Installed files against package digests, modes, and link targets, honouring
rpm's
.B %verify
attributes (full scans).
.TP
.B exe-unowned
Executables, top-level shared libraries, and setuid, setgid, or file
capability binaries in system paths that no package owns.
.TP
.BR repo-key-changed ", " repo-changed " (emits " repo-insecure )
Package signing keys and repositories added or changed; repositories that
skip signature checks.
.TP
.BR proc-exe " (emits " proc-exe-deleted ", " proc-exe-memfd ", " proc-exe-writable )
Processes running from deleted files, anonymous memory files, or writable or
hidden locations.
.TP
.B proc-preload
.IR /etc/ld.so.preload ,
.B LD_PRELOAD
in process environments, and executable mappings from writable, memory, or
deleted files.
.TP
.B proc-service-shell
Shells and interpreters run by service accounts with a TCP or UDP socket on
standard input or output.
.TP
.BR net-listener " (emits " net-hidden ", " net-listener-unowned )
Listening sockets that
.I /proc/net
hides from the kernel's own socket list, and listeners served by binaries no
package owns.
.TP
.B net-raw-socket
Packet and raw IP sockets held by unexpected processes.
.TP
.BR persistence " (emits " persist-new ", " persist-changed )
New or changed systemd units and generators, cron and at jobs, shell startup
files, udev and modprobe rules, PAM configuration and modules, sudoers, sshd
configuration, loader configuration, package manager and initramfs hooks,
and per-user equivalents.
.TP
.BR accounts " (emits " account-uid0 ", " account-password-empty ", " account-new ", " sudo-nopasswd )
Extra uid-0 accounts, empty passwords, new login accounts, and new
passwordless sudo rules.
.TP
.B ssh-key-new
SSH keys authorized for any account, by fingerprint, and
.BR AuthorizedKeysCommand .
.TP
.BR anti-forensics " (emits " history-devnull ", " log-truncated )
History files linked to
.IR /dev/null ;
login and authentication logs truncated in place.
.TP
.B proc-hidden
Processes the kernel runs that the
.I /proc
listing omits, from a BPF task iterator.
.TP
.BR ima-verify " (emits " ima-mismatch )
Files the kernel's IMA measurement list records with content other than
their package's, where a measurement policy is active (for example
.BR ima_policy=tcb ).
The list is append-only for the boot, so a file changed and changed back
still shows. Files whose package was installed during the boot, and files
rpm does not verify the digest of, are not compared.
.TP
.BR flatpak-verify " (emits " flatpak-modified ", " flatpak-missing )
Deployed flatpak apps and runtimes of the system installation against their
ostree commits: files changed, replaced, added, or missing, and symlinks
retargeted (full scans). Objects are hashed when their ctime is newer than
the newest deployment the previous scan saw, or in the rolling sample.
.TP
.B flatpak-signatures
Deployed flatpak commits from a remote that requires signatures, checked
with
.BR gpgv (1)
against the remote's keyring. Commits from OCI remotes carry no signature.
.TP
.BR snap-verify " (emits " snap-modified ", " snap-unasserted )
Installed snaps against the store's
.B snap-revision
assertions: the size always, the SHA3-384 when the file's ctime moved or in
the rolling sample (full scans). Snaps installed from a file without
assertions are
.BR snap-unasserted .
.TP
.B snap-mounts
Each
.I /snap/NAME/REV
mounted from its own
.I .snap
file, still present.
.TP
.BR ostree-verify " (emits " ostree-modified ", " ostree-missing )
On an ostree-booted system, the booted deployment's
.I /usr
against its commit (full scans). It replaces
.B pkg-verify
there.
.TP
.B ostree-deployment
On an ostree-booted system: the image or ref the deployment follows
changing, image transports without signature verification, a staged
deployment of something else, an unlocked
.IR /usr ,
the composefs image changing after deployment, and packages layered on, or
removed from, the image.
.TP
.BR sysext " (emits " sysext-changed )
System and configuration extensions merged over
.IR /usr ,
.IR /opt ,
or
.IR /etc :
each new one, more severely when it is a directory or an image without
dm-verity, and any later change to its content. Programs an extension
provides are not reported by
.BR exe-unowned .
.SH DAEMON
The daemon loads BPF programs that filter in the kernel and send records
through one ring buffer; its rules judge them in userspace.
Each rule has an id, enabled and configured like a check:
.TP
.BR exec " (emits " exec-memfd ", " exec-deleted ", " exec-writable ", " exec-unowned ", " exec-service-shell )
A program run from an anonymous memory file, from a file already deleted,
from a writable or hidden location or tmpfs, or from a file in a system path
that no package installed; a shell or interpreter started by a service
account with a network socket on standard input or output.
.TP
.B write-sensitive
A file opened for writing, outside a package transaction, that matters by
itself: SSH
.IR authorized_keys ,
.IR /etc/ld.so.preload ,
PAM modules and configuration, sudoers, sshd and loader configuration,
.IR /boot ,
the account files, persistence locations, and system programs and libraries
modified in place or added to
.IR /usr/bin .
Other writes under
.IR /usr ,
.IR /boot ,
and
.I /etc
are recorded in
.I dirty.list
and hashed by the next full scan.
.TP
.B module-load
A kernel module loaded from outside
.IR modules.dep ,
not installed by a package, or unsigned.
.TP
.B kexec-load
A kernel loaded for
.BR kexec (8)
(low severity for a crash kernel).
.TP
.B bpf-load
A tracing, kprobe, or LSM BPF program loaded (systemd's and minsec-id's own
are not reported).
.TP
.B priv-escalation
A process with no uid 0 among its real, effective, and saved uids becoming
root other than by executing a setuid program.
.TP
.B ptrace-attach
A process attaching to another user's process, or writing its memory.
.TP
.B devmem-open
.IR /dev/mem ,
.IR /dev/port ,
or
.I /proc/kcore
opened.
.PP
Writes by the package managers (rpm, dnf, apt, dpkg, PackageKit,
rpm-ostree, \&...) and their scriptlets are not reported.
Daemon findings are logged, journaled, and passed to the hook as they
happen, and the same finding is not reported again within
.B daemon.repeat
(an hour).
.B findings
lists those reported within
.B daemon.recent
(a week) after the scan's open findings, marked
.BR [daemon:\ ...] ,
with how many times and how recently; nothing resolves them, since they
record something that happened.
Accept the expected ones.
.PP
The daemon needs a kernel with BTF and the capabilities
.B CAP_BPF
and
.BR CAP_PERFMON ;
.B lockdown=confidentiality
prevents it from running.
.SH JOURNAL
Findings are sent to journald with
.B SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER=minsec-id
and the fields
.BR MINSEC_CHECK ,
.BR MINSEC_SEVERITY ,
.BR MINSEC_SUBJECT ,
and
.BR MINSEC_FINGERPRINT ,
so
.B journalctl MINSEC_SEVERITY=critical
lists the critical ones.
Severity maps to the syslog priority.
.SH PRIVILEGES
Scans should run as root: reading other users' processes needs
.BR CAP_SYS_PTRACE ,
listing BPF programs and running the task iterator needs
.BR CAP_SYS_ADMIN ,
and many checked files are readable only by root.
An unprivileged scan runs what it can and reports the rest as unavailable.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MINSEC_ID_LOG
Log filter for diagnostics on standard error, in
.B tracing
syntax; the default is
.B warn
.RB ( info
for the daemon).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/minsec-id/minsec-id.toml
Main configuration; see
.BR minsec-id.toml (5).
.TP
.I /etc/minsec-id/conf.d/*.toml
Drop-ins, merged in name order.
.TP
.I /var/lib/minsec-id/state.json
Open findings, baselines, and the last scan.
.TP
.I /var/lib/minsec-id/findings.jsonl
The findings log, rotated at 8 MiB to
.IR findings.jsonl.1 .
.TP
.I /var/lib/minsec-id/verified.bin
Files whose ctime moved after install but that hashed clean, so they are not
re-hashed until it moves again.
.TP
.I /var/lib/minsec-id/dirty.list
System files the daemon saw written outside package transactions; the next
full scan hashes them.
.TP
.IR /var/lib/minsec-id/flatpak-verified.bin ", " ostree-verified.bin ", " snap-verified.bin
The same as
.I verified.bin
for flatpak and ostree objects and for snaps.
.TP
.I /run/minsec-id/minsec-id.sock
The daemon's control socket (root only).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B scan
and
.B verify
exit 0 when nothing is open or different, 2 when findings are open or a file
differs, and 1 on error.
Other commands exit 0 on success and 1 on error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sudo minsec-id scan
sudo minsec-id scan \-\-full \-\-check pkg-verify
sudo minsec-id findings \-\-severity high
sudo minsec-id accept kernel-tainted:P \-\-reason "NVIDIA driver"
sudo minsec-id verify /usr/bin/sudo
sudo minsec-id baseline \-\-reset \-\-check ssh-key-new
sudo systemctl enable \-\-now minsec-id
sudo minsec-id accept exec-writable:/tmp/build/* \-\-reason "CI builds"
journalctl SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER=minsec-id \-p warning
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR minsec-id.toml (5),
.BR minsec (1),
.BR rpm (8),
.BR dpkg (1)
